					<p class="body1" align="left"><span class="body1 style73"> <h2>News archive</h2><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-02-17 | Saturday</font></strong><br><br>Blog rule #4081: Always fact check. The last blog entry noted Rick James&#39; &quot;Super Freak&quot; as the sample source for &quot;Dooninit&quot;, but it was actually the more arguably funkier &quot;Give It To Me Baby&quot; that J-Dilla used. Says ?uestlove:<br /><em><br />&quot;Me and (Dilla) met for the first time in Virginia. I have a documented footage of me and him. Out of boredom, I decided to challenge him. So, we played the &quot;Blind Olympics.&quot; Proof (of D-12) chose three records for me, Frank from Frank&#39;n&#39; Dank chose three records for Dilla. He killed me...we had five minutes to make a beat blind, without knowing the records. At first, I cried foul because both Frank and Proof were Dilla&#39;s friends. So, I chose the records for him. I made sure I chose records with no drums, no loops, nothing. I gave him Rick James&#39; &quot;Street Songs.&quot; He was like &quot;you sure about this,&quot; and he did something that I would never forget. He took the intro to &quot;Give it To Me Baby,&quot; slowed it down to an impossible rate, rearranged the bassline. I wanted to cry that day. The next day, Common heard it and liked it, and it wound up being &quot;Doin&#39; It&quot; on Common&#39;s album.&quot; (<a href="http://rap.about.com/od/intervie2/a/RootsInterview_3.htm">Source</a>)<br /><br /></em><img src="http://www.hhv.de/images/cover6/76857.jpg" alt="" width="247" height="233" /><br />Common - Dooinit (<a href="http://www.sendspace.com/file/mz7sxb">mp3</a>) | Dooinit instrumental (<a href="http://www.sendspace.com/file/mve2r2">mp3</a>)<br /><br />I had to edit and revaluate this post while watching &quot;Little Miss Sunshine&quot;...that&#39;s when it hit me (if you&#39;ve seen the movie). I regret the error and any confusion it may have caused! Ok, I gotta get back to this movie (which I recommend so far).<br /><br />Peace<br />dj daps1<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-02-16 | Friday</font></strong><br><br><img src="http://www.atomic.de/pics/-d_in_motion_cover/eugene%20mcdaniels.jpg" alt="" width="170" height="170" /><br /><br />Eugene McDaniels&#39; &quot;Headless Heroes of the Apocalypse&quot; was and remains to be a gem of a record for vinyl digger&#39;s all over the world. Look up this bad boy on ebay and OG copies go for $50-$100+.<br /><br />The magic contained in this album for beat heads can be summed up with the track &quot;Jagger the Dagger&quot; (<a href="http://www.sendspace.com/file/z1qbs4" target="_blank">mp3</a>). Ahhh yes...it&#39;s not just your grandfather or your weirded/weeded out uncle&#39;s record anymore.<br /><br />You might&#39;ve also heard the story about McDaniels&#39; penned jawn &quot;Compared to What&quot;, a stinging social commentary written during wartime (oh wait, it&#39;s still wartime). The song itself has been covered numerous times...remember that Mya/Common Coke commercial? Somebody YouTube that for me. <br /><br />Back then, though, a young woman by the name of Roberta Flack covered this song for her first album, <em>First Take:<br /><br /></em><img src="http://www.davids-music.co.uk/acatalog/Robertaflackfirst.jpg" alt="" width="198" height="200" /><br />Roberta Flack - Compared to What (<a href="http://www.sendspace.com/file/1v9l1l">mp3</a>)<br /><br />Flack killed &#39;em softly with her sizzurpy-sweet voice singing McDaniels&#39; words:<br /><br /><em><font face="verdana, geneva, arial" size="-1"><font face="Verdana,Geneva,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif" size="-1"><font face="Verdana,Geneva,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif" size="1">The president, he got his war.<br />Folks don&#39;t know just what it&#39;s for.<br />No one gives us a rhyme or reason,<br />You have one doubt, they call it treason....<br /><br /></font></font></font></em><font face="verdana, geneva, arial" size="-1"><font face="Verdana,Geneva,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif" size="-1"><font face="Verdana,Geneva,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif" size="1">Horns ablaze through this fiery track, Flack&#39;s version is resurrected by our fam, <strong>Native Guns</strong> (Bambu and Kiwi) outta SF/LA via their equally scathing slapper, &quot;Treason&quot;.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.kqed.org/arts/images/blog/barrel-men.jpg" alt="" width="200" height="200" /><br />Native Guns - Treason (<a href="http://www.sendspace.com/file/w23f0u">mp3</a>)<br /><br />You can pick up the Guns&#39; album <em>Barrel Men</em> <a href="http://cdbaby.com/cd/nativeguns">here</a>, which also features appearances by <strong>Geo of Blue Scholars</strong> on &quot;Said It&quot;, and <strong>Sabzi of Blue Scholars/Common Market</strong> behind the boards on &quot;1995&quot;. See how I managed to make that all <strong>MassLine</strong> relevant? *wipes brow*<br /><br />For those playing at home, you could also play the sample game by branching off from Roberta Flack to the Fugees, and from the Fugees back to ATCQ, then going a little more back into time with Little Feat...i&#39;ll let y&#39;all figure that one out for yourselves, since it could be a whole &#39;nother post on its own ;)<br /><br />Be safe this weekend!<br />peace<br />dj daps1</font></font></font><em><font face="verdana, geneva, arial" size="-1"><font face="Verdana,Geneva,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif" size="-1"><font face="Verdana,Geneva,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif" size="1"><br /></font></font></font></em><p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-02-15 | Thursday</font></strong><br><br>the music industry is desperate.&nbsp; period.&nbsp; &lt;- another period.&nbsp; according to soundscam (no typo), overall&nbsp;album sales were way down in &#39;06, and the&nbsp;trend&#39;s been heading steadily in one direction for more than 5 years (2000: 785 million sold; &#39;06: 588 million sold).&nbsp; no homie, no cry - in the words of the most eloquent <strong>pharoahe monch</strong>, &quot;i say fuck &#39;em, suck my dick from the back with a crazy straw, you lazy whore...&quot;&nbsp;&nbsp; hey -- the man&#39;s a genius, aiight.<br /><br />evidently people are not &quot;seeking out&quot; new music the way they used to.&nbsp; it&#39;s no surprise to me,&nbsp;seeing as how MTV, once our most formidable purveyor of music, is now little more than a live stream of <strong>voyeur.com</strong> (haha -- how many of you scrolled over that lookin&#39; for a link?).&nbsp;<br /><br />but the fact is, folks ARE watching alot of TV (and internet video - three of the four majors recently inked deals with <strong>youtube</strong>).&nbsp; shows like <em><strong>The O.C.</strong></em> provided a very stable platform for taste-makers to launch new bands.&nbsp; now that the show&#39;s gone, it looks like <em><strong>grey&#39;s anatomy</strong></em> is lookin&#39; to carry the torch (both shows claim the same music producer, <strong>alexandra patsavas</strong>).&nbsp; <br /><br />i feel bad for bands&nbsp;like <strong>snow patrol</strong> and <strong>the fray</strong>, &#39;cause everybody&#39;s saying they&#39;d be nothing without support of ABC&#39;s hit drama.&nbsp;&nbsp;here&#39;s the worst part: when asked to explain the successful relationship between the&nbsp;show and the music, the producer says &quot;we wanted a sound that reflected what the show is about, which is young people who don&#39;t know what they&#39;re doing.&quot;<br /><br />nice.&nbsp; i think we have a&nbsp;concept for the next CM album...<br /><br />bye now,&nbsp;<br /><br /><strong>RA</strong> (i&#39;m bold)&nbsp;<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-02-13 | Tuesday</font></strong><br><br>peace to fam-lay.<br /><br />apparently, there is a professor at <strong>Princeton University</strong> that has been using Blue Scholars lyrics as part of the curriculum in their class. &nbsp;<br /><br /><strong>Prof. Mark Lewis Taylor</strong>.&nbsp; that&#39;s filthy.&nbsp; thanks Professor Mark.<br /><br />in fact, we hear quite frequently about teachers, profs, and other types of education facilitators using <strong>MassLine material</strong> in the classrooms, and we&#39;d like to hear more.&nbsp; if you or anyone you know is using MassLine music in the classroom, then get yo&#39; behind to the forums and holla at the folkers!<br /><br />oh yea, and word on the street is that the <strong>Grammy&#39;s</strong> were terrible.<br /><br />saba<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-02-12 | Monday</font></strong><br><br>If you&#39;ve been to a <strong>Blue Scholars </strong>show recently, you might&#39;ve heard me drop some info on the <strong>Stop the Killings</strong> campaign to address the current atrocities in the Philippines. It&#39;s similar to the repression in Ethiopia (and a grip of third world countries - especially US-backed dictatorships) that my man <strong>Gabriel Teodros</strong> is addressing on his new album, <strong>Lovework</strong> (album release party 2/24, drops nationally 2/27!).<br /><br />Seriously though - as of today, over <strong>825</strong> people have been assassinated since 2001 for their pro-people views. Many of the deaths have been attributed directly to the Armed Forces of the Philippines (AFP) and elements in the Philippine government.<br /><br />For Filipinos back home, here and elsewhere, these atrocities aren&#39;t just some faraway foreign third world problem. It&#39;s not just teachers, priests, workers, lawyers, journalists, and activists getting killed. These are our sisters, brothers, cousins, uncles, aunts. Family, friends, kasamas. This aint the cause of the week - the political is personal.<br /><br />We&#39;ve been hollering about this for years with very little media attention, and finally now even <a href="http://seattlepi.nwsource.com/national/301772_philippines31.html">mainstream newspapers like the Seattle-PI are reporting the murders</a>.<br /><br />In support of the campaign, I&#39;ll be linking arms with fellow Pinoy emcees and musicians <strong><a href="http://www.myspace.com/powerstruggle" target="_blank" title="Power Struggle">Power Struggl</a>e</strong>, <a href="http://www.myspace.com/rhapsodistas" target="_blank" title="Rhapsodistas"><strong>Rhapsodistas</strong>,</a> <strong>Praxis Roks</strong>, <a href="http://www.myspace.com/echoofbullets" target="_blank"><strong>Echo of Bullets</strong></a>, <a href="http://www.myspace.com/goldasupernova" target="_blank"><strong>Golda Supernova</strong></a>, and <a href="http://www.myspace.com/kiwi" target="_blank"><strong>Kiwi (of Native Guns)</strong></a> this coming weekend (2/17) in San Francisco for a benefit show at SOMArts. Then we&#39;ll be recording a posse cut in support of the Stop the Killings campaign, which will be available for download here soon. I should probably mention that <strong>Sabzi</strong> laid the beat for the track, too.<br /><br />Proceeds from the show will go to Karapatan, a human rights organization. For more info on the STK campaign and Karapatan, go hither:<br /><br /><a href="http://www.cell68.com/stk/">Stop the Killings website</a><br /><a href="http://www.philonline.com.ph/~krptn/index.html">Karapatan</a><br /><em><br />STOP THE KILLINGS show<br />ALL AGES!!!<br />$10 (Proceeds are going to KARAPATAN)<br />SOMArts (934 Brannan St., SF, CA 94103)<br />Show starts at 7:30pm<br /></em><br />Then, as soon as the dates are confirmed, we&#39;ll let you know where you can further support this campaign by catching the...<br /><br /><strong>STOP THE KILLINGS TOUR</strong><br />featuring<br /><strong>Prometheus Brown (Geologic of Blue Scholars)</strong><br />and<br /><strong>Kiwi (of Native Guns)</strong><br />Coming to your town March 2007<br /><br />ibagsak!<br />Geo<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><center><div class="pagenumbers"><A HREF="archive.php?from=215">[&lt;&lt;]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=0">[1]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=5">[2]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=10">[3]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=15">[4]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=20">[5]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=25">[6]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=30">[7]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=35">[8]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=40">[9]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=45">[10]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=50">[11]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=55">[12]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=60">[13]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=65">[14]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=70">[15]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=75">[16]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=80">[17]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=85">[18]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=90">[19]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=95">[20]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=100">[21]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=105">[22]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=110">[23]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=115">[24]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=120">[25]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=125">[26]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=130">[27]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=135">[28]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=140">[29]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=145">[30]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=150">[31]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=155">[32]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=160">[33]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=165">[34]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=170">[35]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=175">[36]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=180">[37]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=185">[38]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=190">[39]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=195">[40]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=20